      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Thrombomodulin (TM), a natural anticoagulant have been implicated in the pathogenesis of coronary artery disease (CAD) thus emphasizing its potential role as a biomarker."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To investigate the role of the TM genetic variants and soluble TM (sTM) plasma levels in Indian population with CAD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "This case-control study involved genotyping of the entire TM gene and sTM levels estimation in 266 subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "None of the four TM genetic variants identified significantly increased CAD risk in the study population. However, further subgroup analysis revealed that in subjects ≤49 years, C1418T variant (Ala455Val substitution) was significantly associated with CAD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The increased CAD risk in subjects ≤49 years due to TM Ala455Val substitution is a promising finding. Further validation on large Indian cohorts is required in order to screen asymptomatic young subjects for CAD risk and to establish the clinical utility of Ala455Val substitution."
        }
      ]},
